  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.23003559"

abstract: >
  Lean 4 formalization of Structural Assurability, a claim-relative theory
  of the structural capacity of a system and its evidence-producing
  environment to make assurance-relevant evidence obtainable by a specified
  evaluator under stated access, cooperation, and resource conditions.
  The formalization distinguishes obtainable evidence, claim-material
  evidence, evidentiary capabilities, and downstream evidentiary sufficiency;
  defines comparative assurability through capability-set containment; and
  supports equivalence, strict dominance, and incomparability without
  requiring a universal scalar score. It also formalizes observational
  indistinguishability, claim-resolution bounds, candidate structural
  properties, separating constructions, access and resource expansion, and
  representative architecture examples. The formalization also establishes
  conditions for transferring claim-resolution results and resolution-failure
  witnesses between abstract and concrete observation models, illustrated by
  finite-world examples concerning precinct-data vintage and deployment accuracy.

